; GENST.PRG  -  GenST 2.25, the editor and macro assembler of HiSoft Devpac 2.25 ("GenST (C) HiSoft 1985-91")
; Devpac source of the program, disassembled by Darren Birks, 23 November 2026.
; Text 49682 bytes, data 6096 bytes, bss 6688 bytes.
;
; This source rebuilds GENST.PRG byte for byte, header, relocation table and all, with
;   vasmm68k_mot -devpac -m68000 -Ftos -tos-flags=1 -o GENST.PRG GENST.S
;
;=======================================================================
; WHAT IT IS
;=======================================================================
; Two programs in one file.  The first part ($0000-$4923) is the editor, "HiSoft Screen Editor Version 1.10":
; a GEM program with one text window, a menu bar and dialogs.  a6 points at the data section all through it,
; so its d(a6) operands are the data section's objects and the BSS variables (the first set of equates below).
; The second part, from asm_entry at $4924, is the assembler, "GenST Macro Assembler", which the editor runs as
; a child process: Pexec 5 makes a basepage whose text base is $4924, Pexec 4 starts it.  The assembler sets a6
; to its own BSS + 2 and keeps all its variables there (the second set of equates), its stack at
; asm_stack_top(a6) and its heap above.  The editor passes the source in memory through its own basepage +$80:
; 0,"AMP", the source's start and end, the error list, and the buffer for assembling to memory.  Run from the
; desktop with a command line, the assembler works on files (startup sets assembler_linked when the
; basepage's data start is not $4924: an editor-only build reaches a resident assembler through trap #10).
;
; The editor:
;   - The menus are Desk, File (Clear, Load, Save, Save As, Print Block, Insert File, Delete File, Quit), Search
;     (Find, Find Next/Previous, Replace, Replace All), Options (Goto Line, Goto Top/Bottom, Preferences) and
;     Program (Assemble, Run, Debug, MonST, Run with GEM, Jump to Error, Run Other).  Alt and a letter gives the
;     menu commands through alt_key_table; Help shows the keys and the free memory.
;   - The keys are WordStar's: ^S ^D ^E ^X the cursor, ^R ^C page up and down, ^A ^F word left and right, ^Y
;     delete the line, ^Q delete to its end, ^U undo the deleted line, ^T preferences, Esc redraw.  The cursor
;     keys become those codes.  F1/F2 mark the block's start and end, F3 saves it, F4 copies it, F5 pastes it;
;     Shift-F4 remembers it, Shift-F5 deletes it.
;   - The text is one buffer of CR-ended lines (CR LF and LF become CR on loading, CR LF is written back), lines
;     up to 250 characters.  Find matches case or not by patching the first word of a copy of its compare
;     routine (case_fold_code) to rts; get_event checks that copy has not been overwritten.
;   - The preferences (tab 2-16, text buffer size, numeric pad, backups, auto indent, cursor flash, end of line
;     stop or wrap, load MonST) are saved with the Assembly Options and Run settings in the 30-byte GENST2.INF.
;     The defaults are bytes in the program's header ($000a-$0017) that an installer can patch.
;   - Assemble shows the Assembly Options dialog (Shift skips it), builds the assembler's command line from it
;     and runs the assembler; the errors come back in the error list for Jump to Error.  Run and Debug run the
;     program assembled to memory, Debug and MonST through MONST2.PRG loaded at the start ("AMPM" at the
;     basepage's +$80 and the program at +$84 for MonST to find).  Run Other runs any program, "%" in its
;     command line standing for the file's name.
;
; The assembler:
;   - Two passes over the source.  Pass 1 records every optimisation it makes and pass 2 makes only those, so
;     both give the same sizes.  Lines are read from memory or from include files (buffered whole when they fit).
;   - Mnemonics: 158 4-letter names hashed into mnemonic_hash_table, and 24 longer ones searched one by one.
;     Each record gives the opcode word, the handler and how to set the line's label.  The handler gets d6 the
;     opcode, size_code the size (0 none, 1 .B/.S, 2 .W, 3 .L), d1 the first operand character and a4 past it.
;   - The addressing-mode mask word after a call to the operand parser: bits 0-4 Dn An (An) (An)+ -(An),
;     bit 5 d(An) d(An,Xn) and absolute, bit 6 PC-relative, bit 7 immediate; the high byte allows CCR, SR and
;     USP, which return to the mask word + 2.
;   - Expressions: $ hex, % binary, @ octal, 'text', the operators * / << >> & ! | ^ ~ + - and the comparisons
;     = <> != < > <= >=, by an operator stack and a value stack; types relocatable, absolute and imported.
;   - Symbols are binary trees ordered by length and then name: label_tree for relocatable ones, equate_tree
;     for absolute ones, EQUR and REG, a tree of locals per label, and one tree for macros, modules and include
;     files.  Macros (\0-\9, \A-\Z, \@, \#, \<sym>), REPT, IF/ELSEIF/ENDC, INCLUDE, INCBIN, INCDIR, SECTION,
;     RS, OFFSET, ORG, RORG, XDEF/XREF, MODULE, OUTPUT, the listing directives and OPT are handled.
;   - OPT letters (opt_letter_table, A-Z): A auto PC-relative, C symbol length and case, D/X debug symbols,
;     E odd-address check, I '# probably missing', L linkable (L1 GST, L2 DRI), M list macros, O optimising
;     (OW its warnings), P position-independent, S symbol table listing, T type checks, U '_' for local
;     labels, W warnings.
;   - The command line: GENST file -options, with B no binary, C case-independent, D debug, X debug with long
;     names, E name=value symbols, L GST linkable, L2 DRI linkable, M smallest buffers, O<name> the output,
;     P[name] a listing, Q wait for a key at the end, S the symbol table, T<n> the listing's tab width, Z.
;     With no command line it asks for one.
;   - Output: an executable (.PRG, with DRI symbols, 28-byte long names with -X, COMMENT HEAD= for the header
;     flags), GST linkable (.BIN, $FB records) or DRI linkable (.O; COMMENT PASCAL gives the OSS form), or
;     a program in the editor's memory.  The exit code is 0, 5 warnings, 10 errors, 20 fatal, 100 not started.
;   - Errors are numbered from 1 into error_messages, warnings from 1 into msg_warnings.
;
; Some things in it look unfinished or wrong (the comments at each say what the code does):
;   - if_nested_kinds has "GL" where GE belongs, so an IFGE inside a skipped part is not counted.
;   - ifc_compare does not check the second string's length.
;   - A few routines are never called; their comments say so.
;
;=======================================================================
; LAYOUT
;=======================================================================
;   $0000-$1e65  start-up, the GEM calls, the editor's loop and keys, text editing, the Alt commands, find and
;                replace, assembling, running programs and MonST
;   $1e66-$39f9  running other programs, the GEMDOS calls, GEM set-up, the dialogs, the preferences, the event
;                reader, the sliders and status line, loading and saving text
;   $39fa-$4923  the status line and messages, drawing and scrolling the text, the file selector, the block,
;                the Assembly Options dialog's objects
;   $4924-$5439  the assembler: its start, the passes, reading lines, the line parser, symbols, expressions
;   $543a-$65eb  the expression operators and tokens, the operand parser, instructions ABCD-DBcc
;   $65ec-$78f5  the data, symbol, section, OPT and listing directives, instructions EXG-UNLK
;   $78f6-$985b  XDEF/XREF, IF, MACRO, REPT, the mnemonic tables and dispatch, sections, the error messages
;   $985c-$aa69  error reports, optimisations, include files, the symbol listing, the listing file, the output
;                back ends' dispatch, the executable writer
;   $aa6a-$c211  the executable's end, GST and DRI linkable output, the character tables, the assembler's
;                GEMDOS layer, start-up and command line
;   $c212-$d9e1  the editor's resource (dialogs and menu) and strings
;   $d9e2-$f401  the editor's BSS
;
;=======================================================================
